Root cause:
The bug was caused by the `cqspi_setup_flash` function returning success or uninitialized values when it encountered errors while reading the chip select property or when the chip select value was out of range. This lack of proper error handling could lead to undefined behavior or incorrect operation of the driver.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (of_property_read_u32(np, "reg", &cs)) {
```
```c
if (cs >= CQSPI_MAX_CHIPSELECT) {
```
Reason:
The first statement checks for the success of reading the chip select property but does not handle the error properly, potentially leading to uninitialized values being used later. The second statement checks if the chip select value is out of range but does not return a proper error code, which can result in undefined behavior if the value is invalid.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
ret = of_property_read_u32(np, "reg", &cs);
if (ret) {
```
```c
ret = -EINVAL;
```
Reason:
The first statement captures the return value of the property read operation, allowing for proper error handling if it fails. The second statement assigns a specific error code (-EINVAL) when the chip select value is out of range, ensuring that the function returns a meaningful error instead of an uninitialized value.


